<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isparta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isparta is a city in the Lakes Region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, capital of the province of the same name. In the 14th century it was one of the main towns of the Hamidid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> principality that ruled the lake district, before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. It is known for growing roses and producing rose oil, and for its carpets.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isparta.jpg" alt="Isparta" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Isparta at Turkish Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DaglardanIsparta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Isparta is a Turkish form of the Greek name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. The ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Baris</a> in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a> was once said to correspond to it, a later theory identified it with the Eastern Roman fortress Saporda, and Muslim sources call it Sabarta. The archaeologist G. E. Bean found these shifting conceptions bewildering, and modern scholars place Baris near Kılıç in the Keçiborlu district of the province. At an early stage the city became a Christian bishopric, suffragan of the metropolitan see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> of Pisidia. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> are known with certainty: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, who took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325, and Leo, at the Second Council of Nicaea in 787.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> conquered the area in 1071, and in the late 13th century it became part of the Hamidids. In 1381 the Hamidid emir sold Isparta to the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>. Muslim refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> settled around Isparta in the late 19th century, and Bulgarian refugees brought the knowledge of kazanlik rosewater production, which led to its nickname, the city of roses. According to the Ottoman statistics of 1914 most of the district's people were Muslims, with Greek and Armenian minorities. In the middle of the 19th century the city had around 1,000 Greek Orthodox Christian families, and in 1923 its Greek inhabitants were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> under the Greco-Turkish population exchange.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Isparta is the seat of Isparta Province, at an elevation of 1,035 metres, with 247,580 inhabitants in 2022. Its main economic activities are the production of rosewater and handmade carpets, which were a major industry in the early 20th century. The lakes around the city moderate a hot-summer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, and Süleyman Demirel University is based there.</p><p>The city lies close to a fault line, and an earthquake in 1914 destroyed most of the old city, leaving few historical buildings. The oldest is the Kutlu Bey Mosque, or Ulu Camii, built in 1429 by Kutlu Bey, a general of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, which was badly damaged in 1914 and restored in 1922. The Firdevs Paşa Mosque of 1561 and its neighbouring bedesten are attributed to the architect Sinan. The Greek Orthodox church of Aya Baniya, built in 1750, houses a museum of scents.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isparta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Felek al-Din Dundar Bey, grandson of Hamid Bey, a Turkmen commander of the Seljuks, established himself around Egirdir and <b>Isparta</b> at the beginning of the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamidids">Hamidids</a></li></ul></section>
